```java
@SpringBootTest
@EmbeddedKafka(partitions = 1, topics = {"order-events"})
class OrderConsumerTest {
    @Autowired KafkaTemplate<String, OrderEvent> template;
    @Autowired OrderRepository repository;

    @Test
    void shouldProcessOrderEvent() {
        template.send("order-events", "ORD-1", new OrderEvent("ORD-1", BigDecimal.TEN));

        await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
               .untilAsserted(() -> {
                   assertThat(repository.findByOrderId("ORD-1")).isNotNull();
               });
    }

    @Test
    void shouldRouteToDeadLetterOnInvalidEvent() {
        template.send("order-events", "BAD", new OrderEvent(null, null));

        ConsumerRecord<String, OrderEvent> dlt =
            KafkaTestUtils.getSingleRecord(dltConsumer, "order-events.DLT",
                Duration.ofSeconds(10));
        assertThat(dlt.key()).isEqualTo("BAD");
    }
}
```

`partitions = 1` ensures deterministic ordering — multiple partitions introduce non-deterministic assignment that makes assertions about order unreliable. `KafkaTestUtils.getSingleRecord()` waits for one record on the specified topic, providing clean DLT verification.

For producer tests, use a test consumer to verify the message was sent with the correct key, value, headers, and partition. `KafkaTestUtils.getRecords()` returns all records from a topic, useful for batch verification.

`@SpringBootTest` is appropriate for tests that verify **cross-cutting behavior** — how the full application hangs together.

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class ApplicationSmokeTest {
    @Autowired TestRestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Test
    void healthEndpointReturnsUp() {
        ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate.getForEntity("/actuator/health", String.class);
        assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
    }
}
```

`RANDOM_PORT` starts the actual HTTP server — not MockMvc — on a random port. This tests the full HTTP stack including filters, exception handlers, and content negotiation. Use this for smoke tests, security integration tests, and scenarios where MockMvc's simulated request pipeline doesn't catch the issue.

`MOCK` (default) doesn't start a real server but provides a mock servlet environment. Combined with `@AutoConfigureMockMvc`, this gives you `MockMvc` in a full context — useful when you need all beans loaded but don't need a real HTTP server.

Reserve `@SpringBootTest` for the 10-20% of tests that genuinely need the full context. The other 80% should use slices.
